WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.456
DNS maps names to services and publishes supporting information.

00:00:04.456 --> 00:00:12.732
An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address, while an AAAA record maps it to IPv6.

00:00:12.732 --> 00:00:20.159
A CNAME record aliases one name to another, often revealing a hosted platform or service dependency.

00:00:20.159 --> 00:00:22.918
MX records identify mail exchangers.

00:00:22.918 --> 00:00:26.101
NS records identify authoritative name servers.

00:00:26.101 --> 00:00:33.528
TXT records carry text used for purposes such as domain verification, email-security policy, and service configuration.

00:00:33.528 --> 00:00:39.682
A TXT value should be interpreted according to its specific standard and context.

00:00:39.682 --> 00:00:46.260
Together, records can describe public service names, mail providers, verification relationships, and third-party dependencies.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:52.414
They can also expose stale configurations or inconsistent ownership that deserves internal review.

00:00:52.414 --> 00:00:54.324
Interpret DNS cautiously.

00:00:54.324 --> 00:01:02.600
Records may be cached, changed, split by resolver location, fronted by a proxy, or delegated to a provider.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:08.753
One destination can serve many customers, and one service can use many addresses.

00:01:08.753 --> 00:01:15.756
A record is an observed configuration, not automatic proof of who operates the underlying system.

00:01:15.756 --> 00:01:17.241
Time matters.

00:01:17.241 --> 00:01:25.093
Note when the record was observed and whether the source represents current, historical, passive, or authoritative data.

00:01:25.093 --> 00:01:33.793
Independent sources with different collection paths can increase confidence, but repeated copies of one record are not independent corroboration.

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:40.796
Defensive analysis should reconcile public DNS with approved asset inventories, service owners, and vendor records.

00:01:40.796 --> 00:01:49.496
The useful outcome is a documented question or remediation item, not an unsupported conclusion based on a single lookup.
